<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaduna State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaduna State is a state of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with its capital at Kaduna, the former administrative centre of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>. It took its present name in 1976 and its present borders in 1987, when Katsina State was separated from it. It contains the historic city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Zazzau, and has a mixed Muslim and Christian population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaduna-state.jpg" alt="Kaduna State" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Profoss - Original work:Uwe Dedering · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nigeria_-_Kaduna.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kaduna State lies on the northern high plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> savanna country of scattered short trees, shrubs and grasses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-river">Kaduna River</a>, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, flows through it. The most widespread explanation of the name is that it comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> plural kadduna, crocodiles, which were once numerous in the river; another derives it from the Gbagyi name Odna for the river. The state borders seven others, among them Zamfara, Katsina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> to the north and Niger to the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area holds sites of some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s oldest civilisations, among them the Nok civilisation, which prospered from about 1500 BCE to about 500 CE. In the 9th century the geographer and historian Ya'qubi documented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms, which lasted until the region was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> in the early 1800s. Zazzau, the traditional state around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>, is said to have been founded in 1536, and Zaria is said to have been renamed after the younger sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-amina">Queen Amina</a>. The walls built in the time of Queen Amina are between 14 and 16 km long and are closed by eight gates.</p><p>Under British rule Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> moved the capital of the Northern Region from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zungeru">Zungeru</a> to the city of Kaduna in 1916. In 1967 the old Northern Region was divided, and Kaduna became the capital of North-Central State, which was renamed Kaduna State in 1976. In 1987 the state was divided again, creating Katsina State. The state is thus the successor of the old Northern Region, and the many peoples within it have often been thought of simply as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is the city of Kaduna. About 59 to 63 ethnic groups live in the state, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> the dominant ones; the main religions are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and some minority groups, mostly in the south, practise traditional worship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a> are the main crops. The state calls itself the Centre of Learning because of institutions such as Ahmadu Bello University at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>, established in 1962, and the Nigerian Defence Academy. The palace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Zaria, the Zaria city walls and Lugard Hall are among its historic buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaduna State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nok culture is named after Nok, a village in southern <b>Kaduna State</b> in central Nigeria, near which the first of its sculptures came to light.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nok-culture">Nok culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The emirate became part of the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria, and the Emir of Zazzau still reigns in <b>Kaduna State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaria-emirate">Zaria Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
